WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:04.253
3:07 AM. The ledger service broke once — and every re-run passes.

00:00:04.253 --> 00:00:08.696
Thread interleavings. clocks. random seeds: the cause changes every time you run it.

00:00:08.696 --> 00:00:12.505
ChronoForge records the program under a chaos scheduler. hundreds of times.

00:00:12.506 --> 00:00:15.729
Each run is captured as just its nondeterminism — a few hundred bytes. Red runs broke.

00:00:15.729 --> 00:00:18.698
Open a failing run. It isn't re-run — it's replayed bit-for-bit from its recording.

00:00:18.698 --> 00:00:22.161
The verdict: a lost update on balance — 94% confidence. backed by exact steps.

00:00:22.161 --> 00:00:25.265
Evidence 1 → T2 reads balance = 100 …

00:00:25.265 --> 00:00:28.178
… 2 → T1 sneaks in and writes 20 …

00:00:28.178 --> 00:00:31.821
… 3 → T2 writes its stale 50 over it. 80 + 50 paid out of a 100 balance.

00:00:31.821 --> 00:00:35.753
Step backwards through any instruction — state rebuilt from checkpoints + replay.

00:00:35.753 --> 00:00:39.364
Plot any variable over time: the trace of balance goes 100 → 20 → 50.

00:00:39.364 --> 00:00:42.757
“Who wrote this?” — ⟲ reverse-jumps straight to the instruction that produced the value.

00:00:42.757 --> 00:00:50.076
Set a breakpoint. then reverse-continue to the last time it was hit.

00:00:50.076 --> 00:00:53.716
Happens-before analysis flags the data race — it would even in runs that passed.

00:00:53.716 --> 00:00:59.900
Diff against a passing run: where the schedules split. and the first write that differs.

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:10.210
Deadlocks: the lock-order graph shows the fork cycle that froze the table.

00:01:10.210 --> 00:01:21.084
Single-threaded too: the slice walks back from the crash. through the loop. to rand().

00:01:21.084 --> 00:01:29.829
And real Linux binaries: ptrace records syscalls. patches the vDSO. replays the segfault.

00:01:29.829 --> 00:01:33.946
ChronoForge — record once. replay forever. rewind anywhere.
